1
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:05,466
Now recursion looks nice. We often can write

2
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:08,159
less code to solve a problem, but I can tell

3
00:00:08,271 --> 00:00:11,654
you recursion is not always better. It's not

4
00:00:11,718 --> 00:00:12,904
always the best solution.

5
00:00:13,497 --> 00:00:15,565
And for that, let's have a look at a

6
00:00:15,565 --> 00:00:18,082
recursive implementation of the Fibonacci

7
00:00:18,146 --> 00:00:20,519
sequence, which we already had a look at in

8
00:00:20,551 --> 00:00:22,651
the math algorithm course module.

9
00:00:23,436 --> 00:00:25,793
There we used a plain loop to build up an

10
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:27,861
array of numbers where we then return the

11
00:00:28,069 --> 00:00:30,362
nth element, and that gave us linear time

12
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:32,721
complexity. The bigger the number is we feed

13
00:00:32,721 --> 00:00:34,985
in, the longer it takes, but the time it

14
00:00:34,985 --> 00:00:37,876
takes grows in a linear way. That is how we

15
00:00:37,876 --> 00:00:40,125
solved the Fibonacci problem before.

16
00:00:40,687 --> 00:00:42,196
This, by the way, is also called the

17
00:00:42,293 --> 00:00:45,408
bottom-up approach because we're building up

18
00:00:45,424 --> 00:00:47,062
our solution from the bottom. We're building

19
00:00:47,095 --> 00:00:49,359
up that array from the bottom up to the

20
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:51,222
point we need it, and then we return our

21
00:00:51,222 --> 00:00:51,623
value.

22
00:00:52,876 --> 00:00:55,526
Now, that was a totally fine approach, but

23
00:00:55,526 --> 00:00:57,389
let's now see how we could do this with

24
00:00:57,389 --> 00:00:59,955
recursion. The recursive function for the

25
00:01:00,035 --> 00:01:03,241
Fibonacci algorithm could look like this. We

26
00:01:03,257 --> 00:01:03,850
get our n,

27
00:01:04,379 --> 00:01:06,095
or our number, whatever you want to name it,

28
00:01:06,832 --> 00:01:08,483
and we first of all check if

29
00:01:09,397 --> 00:01:09,525
n—

30
00:01:10,278 --> 00:01:12,138
simply less to type, so I'll stick to that—

31
00:01:12,523 --> 00:01:13,629
if n is

32
00:01:14,206 --> 00:01:15,087
equal to 0

33
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:16,338
or

34
00:01:17,171 --> 00:01:18,662
if n is equal to 1.

35
00:01:19,688 --> 00:01:21,532
So if we're trying to get the first or the

36
00:01:21,532 --> 00:01:23,006
second element in that

37
00:01:23,664 --> 00:01:25,828
Fibonacci sequence, and since I start at

38
00:01:25,844 --> 00:01:28,809
index 0, the first element is n, second

39
00:01:28,809 --> 00:01:29,338
element is n.

40
00:01:30,092 --> 00:01:31,570
If you wanna get one of those first 2

41
00:01:31,683 --> 00:01:33,932
elements, we return 1 because you learned

42
00:01:33,932 --> 00:01:35,635
the Fibonacci sequence is an array that

43
00:01:35,635 --> 00:01:38,575
starts with 1, 1, and then we start adding

44
00:01:38,848 --> 00:01:41,596
more and more values here. That is how it

45
00:01:41,644 --> 00:01:43,444
works, right? That's how the Fibonacci

46
00:01:43,444 --> 00:01:46,432
sequence works. So that is kind of our base

47
00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:48,971
case here. And now we can add a recursive

48
00:01:48,971 --> 00:01:49,453
step.

49
00:01:50,256 --> 00:01:52,296
For that, we call our function again from

50
00:01:52,409 --> 00:01:53,260
inside the function

51
00:01:53,855 --> 00:01:54,867
with n minus 1,

52
00:01:55,558 --> 00:01:56,249
and we add

53
00:01:57,034 --> 00:01:59,672
another function call with n minus 2 to

54
00:01:59,688 --> 00:02:01,458
that. Because in the end, that's how we

55
00:02:01,458 --> 00:02:03,484
derived this sequence here, right?

56
00:02:04,031 --> 00:02:06,834
We have a new number that is simply the

57
00:02:06,834 --> 00:02:08,675
number 2 places in front of it,

58
00:02:09,418 --> 00:02:12,114
n minus 2, plus the number 1 place in front

59
00:02:12,114 --> 00:02:13,390
of it, n minus 1.

60
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:15,357
So if we feed in, let's say,

61
00:02:17,721 --> 00:02:20,311
3 here, we want to get the 4th element

62
00:02:20,311 --> 00:02:23,104
because our index starts at 0. So we wanna

63
00:02:23,104 --> 00:02:24,282
get 0, 1,

64
00:02:24,879 --> 00:02:25,073
2,

65
00:02:25,750 --> 00:02:28,203
3, this element, the 3 here indeed.

66
00:02:28,751 --> 00:02:31,591
Well, therefore we would simply not make it

67
00:02:31,591 --> 00:02:34,266
in here because we have n 3, not 0 or 1.

68
00:02:34,798 --> 00:02:36,700
And then we would execute our function for n

69
00:02:36,716 --> 00:02:39,326
minus 1, so for 3 minus 1, which is 2,

70
00:02:39,962 --> 00:02:43,006
plus same function execution for n minus 2,

71
00:02:43,489 --> 00:02:44,584
which is fib of 1.

72
00:02:46,178 --> 00:02:49,143
So these 2 functions will then be kicked off

73
00:02:49,143 --> 00:02:52,242
and will do their thing, which of course for

74
00:02:52,258 --> 00:02:54,728
most numbers will involve that they also

75
00:02:55,049 --> 00:02:56,540
trigger new function calls.

76
00:02:57,358 --> 00:02:59,363
But eventually, once all those function

77
00:02:59,363 --> 00:03:03,196
calls resolve and the result bubbles up, so

78
00:03:03,228 --> 00:03:05,570
to say, and comes back to this main initial

79
00:03:05,634 --> 00:03:06,243
function call,

80
00:03:07,141 --> 00:03:09,563
each of these 2 functions here will return a

81
00:03:09,563 --> 00:03:09,996
value

82
00:03:10,670 --> 00:03:12,755
and then those values will be added together

83
00:03:12,851 --> 00:03:14,968
and this main function call,

84
00:03:15,577 --> 00:03:18,175
in quotes, there is no such thing as a main

85
00:03:18,288 --> 00:03:20,405
function call, but this function call that

86
00:03:20,405 --> 00:03:23,406
started everything will return that overall

87
00:03:23,663 --> 00:03:24,449
value eventually.

88
00:03:25,204 --> 00:03:27,099
That's the idea how we can leverage

89
00:03:27,211 --> 00:03:29,828
recursion to solve this Fibonacci problem.

90
00:03:30,888 --> 00:03:32,157
If we try this,

91
00:03:32,751 --> 00:03:35,689
and if you try this, use a small number, you

92
00:03:35,721 --> 00:03:38,451
will soon see why. So if I try this for 4

93
00:03:38,451 --> 00:03:39,141
and 5

94
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:43,220
and I then reload, I get 5 and 8, which are

95
00:03:43,348 --> 00:03:45,724
the expected numbers in my sequence. 4 is

96
00:03:45,901 --> 00:03:47,442
index 4, so 0,

97
00:03:48,148 --> 00:03:48,292
1,

98
00:03:49,110 --> 00:03:49,302
2,

99
00:03:50,088 --> 00:03:50,393
3,

100
00:03:50,954 --> 00:03:54,964
4, that was 5 as expected. 5 is index 5, so

101
00:03:55,364 --> 00:03:57,513
the next number in line, that was 8 and that

102
00:03:57,610 --> 00:03:59,694
were our results. So this works, what's

103
00:03:59,694 --> 00:04:00,240
wrong with it?

104
00:04:01,058 --> 00:04:03,207
Well, what's wrong with this approach is

105
00:04:03,303 --> 00:04:05,468
that when we call it, we in the end build up

106
00:04:05,468 --> 00:04:08,659
such a tree of nested recursive function

107
00:04:08,691 --> 00:04:09,285
calls here.

108
00:04:09,974 --> 00:04:12,027
In the end, we have a couple of nested

109
00:04:12,123 --> 00:04:13,840
function calls. For 4,

110
00:04:14,546 --> 00:04:17,758
we call fib and fib inside of that function.

111
00:04:17,999 --> 00:04:20,744
And inside of those functions, we call fib,

112
00:04:20,809 --> 00:04:21,178
fib,

113
00:04:21,756 --> 00:04:22,976
or fib, fib,

114
00:04:23,763 --> 00:04:26,878
and maybe even more nested function calls.

115
00:04:27,617 --> 00:04:30,571
And this, of course, doesn't look too good.

116
00:04:30,683 --> 00:04:33,381
We add more and more function calls

117
00:04:33,959 --> 00:04:36,544
for relatively small numbers already.

118
00:04:37,652 --> 00:04:40,284
In addition, you can see we have kind of

119
00:04:40,396 --> 00:04:43,653
repeated function calls. Fib will always

120
00:04:43,749 --> 00:04:46,092
produce the same result. Nonetheless, we're

121
00:04:46,092 --> 00:04:48,498
calling it multiple times here simply

122
00:04:48,498 --> 00:04:51,113
because it's in different branches of this

123
00:04:51,466 --> 00:04:52,589
recursive tree here.

124
00:04:53,809 --> 00:04:56,167
Now for this example here, we have 9

125
00:04:56,504 --> 00:05:00,162
function executions. We call fib, fib, fib.

126
00:05:00,852 --> 00:05:03,258
You can simply add it up. It's 9 boxes which

127
00:05:03,258 --> 00:05:04,317
I have in this tree here.

128
00:05:05,359 --> 00:05:07,762
So we have 9 function executions for the

129
00:05:07,762 --> 00:05:08,739
number 4.

130
00:05:09,973 --> 00:05:14,155
Now this gets worse for fib. This tree would

131
00:05:14,155 --> 00:05:17,487
look like that here. A lot of nested

132
00:05:17,503 --> 00:05:20,868
function calls, 15 executions, even though

133
00:05:20,884 --> 00:05:24,088
we just increased fib to fib. So we just

134
00:05:24,088 --> 00:05:25,402
increased the number by 1.

135
00:05:26,075 --> 00:05:26,844
Nonetheless,

136
00:05:27,437 --> 00:05:29,183
we went from 9 executions

137
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:32,788
all the way up to 15 executions. So we added

138
00:05:32,836 --> 00:05:36,718
6 extra executions for a number increase of

139
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:39,557
1, so from 4 to 5. So the number was only

140
00:05:39,557 --> 00:05:41,707
increased by 1, but we added 6 extra

141
00:05:41,707 --> 00:05:42,332
executions,

142
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:45,621
and we got more repetition here, a lot of

143
00:05:45,653 --> 00:05:48,220
repeated blocks of code in that tree.

144
00:05:49,102 --> 00:05:52,006
And that gets even worse if we go from 5 to

145
00:05:52,006 --> 00:05:54,781
6. So again, we only increase the number by

146
00:05:54,877 --> 00:05:57,220
1. Nonetheless, the tree now looks like

147
00:05:57,284 --> 00:06:00,107
that, and I don't even mark the repeated

148
00:06:00,252 --> 00:06:02,273
blocks. You will see a lot of repeated

149
00:06:02,337 --> 00:06:04,423
branches, a lot of repeated function calls

150
00:06:04,423 --> 00:06:07,086
in there. And here we have 25 code

151
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:07,840
executions.

152
00:06:08,834 --> 00:06:12,139
So we go from 9 executions for fib for the

153
00:06:12,139 --> 00:06:12,716
number 4

154
00:06:13,647 --> 00:06:16,454
to 15 executions if we just increase that

155
00:06:16,454 --> 00:06:17,706
number by 1 to 5,

156
00:06:18,283 --> 00:06:21,331
to even 25 executions if we increase it

157
00:06:21,331 --> 00:06:25,486
again by 1 from 5 to 6. So we add more and

158
00:06:25,502 --> 00:06:28,343
more executions for every increase in the

159
00:06:28,343 --> 00:06:31,312
number we feed in, and that's a problem.

160
00:06:31,954 --> 00:06:34,699
So what would be our time complexity of this

161
00:06:34,891 --> 00:06:38,712
recursive function? It's probably not O,

162
00:06:39,161 --> 00:06:41,906
which was the time complexity for the

163
00:06:42,837 --> 00:06:46,448
loop-based solution. There we had O, but

164
00:06:46,448 --> 00:06:47,781
what's the time complexity here?

165
00:06:48,872 --> 00:06:50,895
Well, we can of course count.

166
00:06:51,745 --> 00:06:53,591
As mentioned multiple times, we got 9

167
00:06:53,591 --> 00:06:54,602
executions for 4,

168
00:06:55,679 --> 00:06:57,045
Got 15 for 5,

169
00:06:57,944 --> 00:06:59,712
got 25 for 6.

170
00:07:00,451 --> 00:07:03,729
So if we increase the number we feed to the

171
00:07:03,729 --> 00:07:04,966
function by just 1,

172
00:07:05,722 --> 00:07:07,473
we have an exponential increase in

173
00:07:07,570 --> 00:07:10,349
executions. It doesn't grow in a linear way.

174
00:07:10,703 --> 00:07:13,917
It's not like the number goes from 5 to 6

175
00:07:13,933 --> 00:07:16,793
and we have 1 extra new execution. No, in

176
00:07:16,905 --> 00:07:17,355
this case,

177
00:07:17,934 --> 00:07:22,013
from 5 to 6, we go from 15 to 25. So we have

178
00:07:22,062 --> 00:07:23,618
10 extra executions,

179
00:07:24,372 --> 00:07:26,346
and that was a bigger increase than we had

180
00:07:26,362 --> 00:07:26,812
from 4

181
00:07:27,822 --> 00:07:28,914
with 9 executions

182
00:07:29,732 --> 00:07:33,599
to 5 with 15 executions. So the number of

183
00:07:33,599 --> 00:07:35,685
executions we add seems to grow

184
00:07:36,231 --> 00:07:38,991
the bigger our n gets, and it grows in an

185
00:07:39,280 --> 00:07:40,596
exponential way.

186
00:07:41,543 --> 00:07:46,132
Now I'm not talking about O, about quadratic

187
00:07:46,132 --> 00:07:49,051
time, because if we had a quadratic time

188
00:07:49,083 --> 00:07:50,494
complexity, that would mean

189
00:07:51,247 --> 00:07:53,924
that if our n is, let's say,

190
00:07:54,533 --> 00:07:55,175
6,

191
00:07:55,976 --> 00:07:59,294
we would simply have 6 times 6, 36

192
00:07:59,903 --> 00:08:02,340
operations if we had quadratic time.

193
00:08:03,173 --> 00:08:05,097
And if we then go to 7, we would have 7

194
00:08:05,113 --> 00:08:08,014
times 7, so we would only add 13 new

195
00:08:08,222 --> 00:08:08,992
operations here.

196
00:08:09,986 --> 00:08:12,775
Now the problem is that this doesn't seem to

197
00:08:12,807 --> 00:08:15,067
be the kind of growth we have in our

198
00:08:15,436 --> 00:08:17,952
function here because if I check this for

199
00:08:18,497 --> 00:08:21,287
different n's, for different numbers, you

200
00:08:21,287 --> 00:08:24,509
see the growth is moderate. Of course, we go

201
00:08:24,509 --> 00:08:27,073
from 16 to 25 to 36

202
00:08:27,618 --> 00:08:30,888
to 49, but the jump isn't as large.

203
00:08:31,706 --> 00:08:33,966
Now I can tell you, it just doesn't fit on a

204
00:08:33,966 --> 00:08:34,463
slide.

205
00:08:35,505 --> 00:08:37,990
If we would go from fib to fib,

206
00:08:38,695 --> 00:08:41,083
we would not just add 10 or 12 new

207
00:08:41,164 --> 00:08:41,837
executions,

208
00:08:42,366 --> 00:08:44,710
No, we would pretty much double the amount

209
00:08:44,710 --> 00:08:47,488
of executions here. So quadratic time is too

210
00:08:47,697 --> 00:08:50,459
slow. We're not growing with quadratic time

211
00:08:50,507 --> 00:08:53,333
here. I can tell you that our algorithm here

212
00:08:53,686 --> 00:08:57,154
actually has exponential time complexity, O

213
00:08:57,459 --> 00:09:00,446
of Q at the power of N. So not quadratic

214
00:09:00,446 --> 00:09:03,256
time, that would be N at the power of 2, but

215
00:09:03,336 --> 00:09:05,905
Q at the power of N. Because the bigger the

216
00:09:05,905 --> 00:09:07,109
number is you feed in here,

217
00:09:08,137 --> 00:09:09,567
the longer it will take.

218
00:09:10,241 --> 00:09:12,731
Now, how do I derive this though?

219
00:09:13,373 --> 00:09:15,798
Well, for one, you could simply test it for

220
00:09:16,328 --> 00:09:18,882
bigger numbers and see how many operations

221
00:09:18,978 --> 00:09:19,524
you're getting.

222
00:09:20,103 --> 00:09:21,323
But you can also have a look at the

223
00:09:21,387 --> 00:09:24,021
function, and what you see is that for every

224
00:09:24,054 --> 00:09:27,121
function execution, we start 2 new

225
00:09:27,651 --> 00:09:30,221
function executions, right? We have this

226
00:09:30,221 --> 00:09:33,016
return statement where we have 2 recursive

227
00:09:33,112 --> 00:09:33,947
calls in the end.

228
00:09:34,492 --> 00:09:37,345
So since we start 2 new function executions

229
00:09:37,441 --> 00:09:39,107
for every execution,

230
00:09:39,684 --> 00:09:42,312
that's exactly this exponential growth.

231
00:09:42,985 --> 00:09:45,677
We introduce 2 new function calls for every

232
00:09:45,709 --> 00:09:46,382
function call,

233
00:09:46,911 --> 00:09:49,651
so every function call then again spins up 2

234
00:09:49,732 --> 00:09:52,247
new function calls, and that quickly

235
00:09:52,424 --> 00:09:54,891
explodes and gives us a lot of ongoing

236
00:09:54,924 --> 00:09:57,279
function calls. And that's what we call

237
00:09:57,552 --> 00:09:59,939
exponential growth. When the growth

238
00:10:00,212 --> 00:10:02,861
basically accelerates itself, you could say.

239
00:10:03,118 --> 00:10:04,740
And that's exactly what we have here.

240
00:10:05,671 --> 00:10:08,529
And you will relatively soon find a number

241
00:10:08,802 --> 00:10:11,130
where this actually crashes your browser.

242
00:10:11,837 --> 00:10:13,908
If I try this for 35—

243
00:10:14,502 --> 00:10:16,349
and depending on your machine, you might not

244
00:10:16,349 --> 00:10:17,248
want to try this—

245
00:10:17,954 --> 00:10:20,090
you will already see that this at least is

246
00:10:20,122 --> 00:10:22,113
not there instantly. It takes a bit longer

247
00:10:22,113 --> 00:10:25,212
than a 5. If I try it for 37 here

248
00:10:26,384 --> 00:10:28,405
You already see now we have a significant

249
00:10:28,469 --> 00:10:30,812
gap here, a significant pause before this

250
00:10:30,812 --> 00:10:33,362
appears. So this takes longer and longer for

251
00:10:33,362 --> 00:10:36,010
very small increases here. And this, I can

252
00:10:36,026 --> 00:10:38,256
tell you, is not quadratic time. For

253
00:10:38,256 --> 00:10:40,502
quadratic time, you would have no problem

254
00:10:40,502 --> 00:10:41,705
with such a small number.

255
00:10:42,267 --> 00:10:45,074
This indeed is exponential time complexity,

256
00:10:45,379 --> 00:10:47,529
because we do not just add one function call

257
00:10:47,641 --> 00:10:50,160
with every increase in n, but we add whole

258
00:10:50,272 --> 00:10:53,227
new branches this recursive tree, and every

259
00:10:53,339 --> 00:10:55,572
branch consists of other branches, and

260
00:10:55,572 --> 00:10:58,850
therefore this quickly grows to a number

261
00:10:59,203 --> 00:11:01,276
that your computer won't be able to handle.

262
00:11:01,999 --> 00:11:03,814
And therefore, this of course is a horrible

263
00:11:03,862 --> 00:11:04,376
solution

264
00:11:04,971 --> 00:11:07,911
for a problem we already solved with linear

265
00:11:08,023 --> 00:11:11,076
time complexity. This is an example for a

266
00:11:11,076 --> 00:11:13,469
recursive function that's not better than

267
00:11:13,502 --> 00:11:17,293
the loop-based solution. This takes far

268
00:11:17,582 --> 00:11:21,075
longer. It's far slower. It's not a good

269
00:11:21,075 --> 00:11:23,330
solution for this problem. It might look

